 \begin{proof}
 This can be reduced to the familiar case that $f$ is indeed a function into $\VF^m$ as follows. By Lemma~\ref{finite:VF:project:RV} and compactness, there are a definable set $U$ in $\RV$ and a definable function $g: A \times \VF^m \fun A \times U$ such that, for all $a \in A$, $\pr_A(g(a, b)) = a$  and $g \rest (a \times f(a))$ is injective. For each $t \in U$, let
 \[
 A_t = \set{a \in A \given (a,t) \in g(a \times f(a))}
 \]
-and $f_t \sub f$ be the obvious $t$-definable function $A_t \fun \VF^m$. By dimension theory, more precisely, by Lemma~\ref{full:dim:open:poly}, $\dim_{\VF}(A_t) = n$ for some $t \in U$. For such a $t \in U$, by Lemma~\ref{fun:almost:cont}, $f_t$ is continuous almost everywhere.  The claim follows.
+and $f_t \sub f$ be the obvious $t$-definable function $A_t \fun \VF^m$. The discontinuity locus $E$ of $f$ is definable: the limit condition is first-order, and minimality of the finite set $f(a)$ can be tested by removing one of its elements at a time. Suppose for contradiction that $\dim_{\VF}(E)=n$. By Lemma~\ref{full:dim:open:poly}, $E$ contains a nonempty open polydisc. Apply generic local constancy \cite[Lemma~8.9]{Yin:special:trans} to the finite label-set map
+\[
